1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,050
Welcome to Module Six, the last module of Unit Two.

2
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:06,087
Here is a hypothesis.

3
00:00:06,087 --> 00:00:08,725
Warmer streams have fewer stoneflies.

4
00:00:08,725 --> 00:00:13,837
It is a perfectly good hypothesis, and it is useless as a set of instructions.

5
00:00:13,837 --> 00:00:17,187
Before anyone goes into the field, you write a protocol.

6
00:00:17,187 --> 00:00:18,412
Which sites.

7
00:00:18,412 --> 00:00:19,612
Which method.

8
00:00:19,612 --> 00:00:20,862
What you will measure.

9
00:00:20,862 --> 00:00:22,612
How many replicates.

10
00:00:22,612 --> 00:00:25,337
And what result would count as an answer.

11
00:00:25,337 --> 00:00:29,850
Hand a field team only the hypothesis, and they will come back with something.

12
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:31,850
It probably will not be what you needed.

13
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,800
A prompt to a coding agent is a protocol.

14
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:42,725
And you are handing it to a team that is extremely fast, extremely willing, and never asks a clarifying question unless you tell it to.

15
00:00:42,725 --> 00:00:44,550
So what makes a good one?

16
00:00:44,550 --> 00:00:47,712
One of today's guides boils it down to three parts.

17
00:00:47,712 --> 00:00:50,912
A goal, a scope, and a done criterion.

18
00:00:50,912 --> 00:00:52,925
What should be true at the end.

19
00:00:52,925 --> 00:00:55,925
What the agent may and may not touch.

20
00:00:55,925 --> 00:00:57,937
And how you will know it worked.

21
00:00:57,937 --> 00:01:00,037
That last part matters most.

22
00:01:00,037 --> 00:01:01,325
Tests pass.

23
00:01:01,325 --> 00:01:03,675
The output matches a reference file.

24
00:01:03,675 --> 00:01:05,675
Something a command can check.

25
00:01:05,675 --> 00:01:09,000
Without it, done just means the agent thinks it is done.

26
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,962
The creator of Claude Code adds two habits.

27
00:01:11,962 --> 00:01:17,537
First, ask the agent to make a plan and wait for your approval before it writes any code.

28
00:01:17,537 --> 00:01:19,725
Fixing a plan takes seconds.

29
00:01:19,725 --> 00:01:22,550
Fixing the wrong code takes much longer.

30
00:01:22,550 --> 00:01:26,487
Second, give it a way to check its own work, like a test it can run.

31
00:01:26,487 --> 00:01:29,762
With feedback, it improves on each pass.

32
00:01:29,762 --> 00:01:31,912
He also has advice for day one.

33
00:01:31,912 --> 00:01:34,300
Start with questions, not edits.

34
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:41,050
Ask the agent how a piece of code is used, or why a function looks so strange, and let it dig through the history.

35
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:44,737
Nothing changes, and you learn where its abilities end.

36
00:01:44,737 --> 00:01:47,975
Another of today's writers suggests a trick I like.

37
00:01:47,975 --> 00:01:50,612
Do not write a five-hundred-word prompt.

38
00:01:50,612 --> 00:01:57,137
Write one line of intent and the criteria for success, then say, ask me questions before you start.

39
00:01:57,137 --> 00:02:02,187
You answer a few multiple-choice questions, and the gaps in your own thinking show up early.

40
00:02:02,187 --> 00:02:04,287
Now, where does the analogy break?

41
00:02:04,287 --> 00:02:09,125
A field assistant who receives a vague protocol will usually come and ask.

42
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:12,475
A reviewer might catch the problem before the season starts.

43
00:02:12,475 --> 00:02:17,450
A coding agent fills every gap with a confident guess and begins immediately.

44
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:20,750
If you want questions and a plan, you have to ask for them.

45
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:28,612
In today's lab you will take three prompts you really sent recently, rewrite them as protocols, and run the old and new versions side by side.

46
00:02:28,612 --> 00:02:30,937
You will score each answer out of three.

47
00:02:30,937 --> 00:02:40,250
Then you will finish Unit Two by adding a short, how-to-work-here section to your capstone's map, so every future session starts with your protocol already in place.

48
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:43,412
Open your terminal, and let's write better protocols.

